The 6-generator LOGO average macro-F1 is \textbf{0.789}, with per-generator scores ranging from 0.683 to 0.856. Five of six folds exceed 0.76, and the within-family pair Pythia-2.8B / Pythia-1B shows that the classifier does not merely memorize family-specific artifacts (F1 0.804 and 0.856, respectively). A bidirectional family-out analysis on the 5-generator (${\leq}$2.8B) pool confirms cross-family transfer: training on non-Pythia generators and testing on Pythia yields F1\,=\,0.77, and the reverse yields F1\,=\,0.76, both with moderate degradation (${\sim}$3.7\,pp below LOGO).

The clear outlier is Qwen3-8B (8.2B parameters), whose held-out F1 of 0.683 falls well below the 5-generator average of 0.800 obtained before adding this generator to the pool. This cross-scale transfer gap suggests that substantially larger models produce text whose depth fingerprint differs from the sub-2B generators used in training---a finding we return to in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

Per-class analysis reveals that gen-0 is classified near-perfectly (F1\,=\,0.94), gen-2+ reliably (0.80), while gen-1 remains the most challenging class (0.64). The gen-0 result is consistent with the well-documented distributional gap between human and machine text \cite{detectgpt,binoculars}. The lower gen-1 F1 reflects the subtler distinction between first-generation and deeper text, where the drift signal has not yet fully accumulated.

Figure~\ref{fig:logo_performance} visualizes per-generator performance with class-level breakdowns.

We construct non-paraphrastic generation chains using 6 generators spanning 3 model families: \textbf{Pythia} \cite{pythia} (Pythia-2.8B, Pythia-1B), \textbf{OLMo} \cite{olmo} (OLMo-2-1B), and \textbf{Qwen} \cite{qwen3} (Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B). This selection covers a parameter range from 0.6B to 8.2B and ensures that cross-generator generalization is evaluated across architecturally distinct model families.

For each generator, we sample ${\sim}$1{,}000 seed passages from English Wikipedia as depth-0 (human-written) text. Each seed is extended through a 4-step continuation chain: the prompt \texttt{"Continue the following text:"} is applied iteratively to produce depths gen-1 through gen-4. All generations use temperature $0.7$, top-$p$ sampling at $0.9$ \cite{nucleus_sampling}, and a maximum of 256 new tokens. The continuation (non-paraphrastic) setting preserves the sequential generation context without the semantic-preservation constraint of paraphrasing, which prior work shows bounds depth signal to ${\sim}$3 classes due to attractor convergence \cite{markovian_chains}. Natural dropout at deeper depths yields ${\sim}$23K total records. For classification, we collapse depths 2--4 into a single $2{+}$ class, producing a 3-class ordinal target.

